    text-split-limit-ignored, P2, open, "text.split(sep, N) silently ignores the limit arg N and always splits on every occurrence of sep (repro: 'a:b:c'.split(':', 2) returns 3 parts [a,b,c] instead of splitn's [a, b:c]); LIVE root cause is the Rust seed's method dispatch (src/compiler_rust/compiler/src/interpreter_method/string.rs:199 and interpreter_helpers/method_dispatch.rs:85), both of which read only args[0] — NOT Rust-fixable per repo rule (never edit Rust seed code), and the currently-deployed bin/simple IS this seed binary (confirmed via seed_warning banner + binary inspection), so the bug is observable today via bin/simple run/test; the self-hosted .spl interpreter (eval_methods.spl) had the identical bug and has been given a splitn-semantics fix in .spl (keep first N-1 parts, rejoin the remainder with sep) — unverified live since this path is dormant until a self-hosted redeploy replaces the pinned seed, so this does NOT flip the bug to fixed; native-build MIR-lowering codegen (method_calls_literals.spl ts_arity_ok gate) also only fast-paths split at args.len()==1 and remains untouched, a separate larger-scope gap; a regression example belongs in test/01_unit/compiler/interpreter/nested_string_split_spec.spl once the interpreter fix is live (not added now — a currently-failing example against the live seed binary would violate the never-add-failing-tests rule)", src/compiler/10.frontend/core/interpreter/eval_methods.spl, 370, N/A (see description for standalone repro), 2026-04-14, 2026-04-14, true
